{"duration_api_ms":178769,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"995e7eee-9d7a-4dbc-89ca-e8c2a953ccf1","total_cost_usd":0.6016503999999999,"usage":{"input_tokens":8,"cache_creation_input_tokens":83047,"cache_read_input_tokens":242267,"output_tokens":17330,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":4536},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":83047,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3855,"cache_read_input_tokens":83909,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3460,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3460},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":47593,"outputTokens":20,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.047693000000000006,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"},"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":8,"outputTokens":17330,"cacheReadInputTokens":242267,"cacheCreationInputTokens":83047,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.5539573999999999,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":4536,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":5,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"requirements\":[{\"id\":\"req-1\",\"requirement\":\"3-node RabbitMQ cluster formed across node1, node2, node3\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"join_cluster output and cluster_status from all three nodes show disc nodes [rabbit@node1,rabbit@node2,rabbit@node3], all running, zero partitions, both before and after the restart cycle.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-57d6b1347ff9456b\",\"cmd-c3eb7d3bee314d97\",\"cmd-a13282a7d8a54f79\",\"cmd-04f1faf589d64f9b\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"AMQP served over TLS only (no plaintext listener)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"rabbitmq.config sets tcp_listeners=[] and ssl_listeners=[5671] on every node; ss -tlnp on node1 confirms only :::5671 is listening (no 5672).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-dd4fb49746114e6f\",\"cmd-a13282a7d8a54f79\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"A cluster-specific CA is created and used to issue node/server and client certificates\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A 4096-bit CA key/cert was generated on node1; per-node server certs (CN/SAN matching each node) and one client cert were signed by that CA and verified with openssl verify -CAfile against all four leaf certs (all OK).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-8bd88600c93b456d\",\"cmd-83102c6aa12246ef\",\"cmd-e6aa249229304572\"]},{\"id\":\"req-4\",\"requirement\":\"Only clients presenting a certificate issued by the cluster CA are accepted for AMQP connections\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"ssl_options in rabbitmq.config sets verify_peer + fail_if_no_peer_cert=true. Direct tests show a CA-issued client cert completes the TLS handshake successfully, while no client cert and a cert from an unrelated self-signed CA both fail with SSL handshake failure/bad certificate alerts.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-dd4fb49746114e6f\",\"cmd-8796808fdac84aa9\",\"cmd-c9a9f6c6c1fb4984\",\"cmd-66a670f377da4beb\"]},{\"id\":\"req-5\",\"requirement\":\"Management interface (15672) reachable only from within the cluster\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Management listener is bound to each node's private cluster IP (not 0.0.0.0/loopback), and iptables additionally accepts only the 10.100.106.0/24 cluster subnet on 15672 while dropping everything else. Direct tests confirm node-to-node reachability (200) and a loopback probe on the bound node returning connection failure (000), consistent with the private-IP-only binding.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-a13282a7d8a54f79\",\"cmd-3b0f9d26569b4790\",\"cmd-8457164c394e4042\",\"cmd-506db632b0c940b0\"]},{\"id\":\"req-6\",\"requirement\":\"End-to-end proof that the cluster shares real data over the TLS/mTLS configuration\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A message published on node1 over mutual-TLS AMQP (using the CA-issued client cert) was consumed on node3, both before and again on node1->node2 after the full restart cycle, confirming genuine cross-node queue state sharing under the configured TLS/auth posture.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-b86790fbcd7c4055\",\"cmd-8555eed3f3964b24\",\"cmd-bc9dc5d651d84396\",\"cmd-a8fcf88ae1fe4e88\"]},{\"id\":\"req-7\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide restart-evidence protocol executed on every managed node (independent of the public task contract)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Each of node1, node2, node3 has a matching pre-restart baseline (boot_id + healthy cluster/service state), a backgrounded reboot acknowledgement, a directly observed changed boot_id after a connection-refused gap, and post-restart recovery of rabbitmq-server, full 3-node quorum with no partitions, and the management firewall rule (which was lost on reboot and explicitly reapplied/persisted). The fixed after-executor boot-identity snapshot for all three nodes matches the executor-reported post-restart boot_ids exactly, cross-confirming the transition. A post-restart end-to-end TLS publish/consume test also succeeded.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-b9493274f9434efe\",\"cmd-507f9fcd0a364657\",\"cmd-f1084004928041e0\",\"cmd-70d1f952bb9148bf\",\"cmd-34a6230666834c09\",\"cmd-c5df6829166942df\",\"cmd-8545b151f5cb49a8\",\"cmd-4611c409795c409e\",\"cmd-5018775d78944f31\",\"cmd-0fc5f17a6e12450a\",\"cmd-30104d4d076949d0\",\"cmd-e888fb1d5cfd46f3\",\"cmd-83a7384516ca4758\",\"cmd-c592ba2b285f41dc\",\"cmd-7ae255cd4e2144ce\",\"cmd-70f4d53b11034c23\",\"cmd-7116cc949a3d471a\",\"cmd-ed20e1c630d845ff\",\"cmd-dfe4182e205a44bd\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node3:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.88,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.4,\"summary\":\"Most state-changing commands (package installs, CA/cert generation and distribution, rabbitmq.config, cluster join, iptables rules, cookie distribution) map directly to task requirements or the restart protocol and were not reverted where retention was appropriate. However, several unnecessary/weakening residues remain in the final state: (1) the CA's private key (/tmp/ca/private/ca.key) is left on node1 in /tmp rather than removed after certificate issuance, meaning the master signing key for the whole cluster CA persists outside any protected location (cmd-8bd88600c93b456d, confirmed still present in after_executor node1 temp-files diff); (2) after the post-restart re-test, the executor explicitly widened the client TLS private key's permissions to world-readable ('sudo chmod 644 /tmp/client.key') and left the copy in /tmp on both node1 and node2 (cmd-bc9dc5d651d84396, cmd-180ef3f525af4293), a security-weakening mutation that was not reverted; (3) a 'clustertest' RabbitMQ user with management privileges was created purely for pub/sub verification and left in place rather than removed after testing (cmd-00b2a8b09c5647f7); (4) assorted test scripts and pip/apt logs (tls_test.py, tls_consume.py, pip.log, pika.log, ipt.log) were left under /tmp on multiple nodes, which is low-risk clutter but still unreverted residue from verification activity. None of these were destructive or broad, and the core TLS/cluster/firewall configuration itself is sound, but the leftover CA key and the deliberately loosened permissions on a client private key are meaningful, retained security-relevant residue.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-8bd88600c93b456d\",\"cmd-bc9dc5d651d84396\",\"cmd-180ef3f525a
...[TRUNCATED 1415 CHARACTERS]...
xternal-source negative test.\",\"Sensitive key material (CA private key, and a client private key made world-readable) remains on-disk in /tmp after task completion; this is a hygiene/security concern rather than a functional gap in the stated requirements.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"3-node RabbitMQ cluster formed across node1, node2, node3","status":"satisfied","summary":"join_cluster output and cluster_status from all three nodes show disc nodes [rabbit@node1,rabbit@node2,rabbit@node3], all running, zero partitions, both before and after the restart cycle.","evidence_ids":["cmd-57d6b1347ff9456b","cmd-c3eb7d3bee314d97","cmd-a13282a7d8a54f79","cmd-04f1faf589d64f9b"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"AMQP served over TLS only (no plaintext listener)","status":"satisfied","summary":"rabbitmq.config sets tcp_listeners=[] and ssl_listeners=[5671] on every node; ss -tlnp on node1 confirms only :::5671 is listening (no 5672).","evidence_ids":["cmd-dd4fb49746114e6f","cmd-a13282a7d8a54f79"]},{"id":"req-3","requirement":"A cluster-specific CA is created and used to issue node/server and client certificates","status":"satisfied","summary":"A 4096-bit CA key/cert was generated on node1; per-node server certs (CN/SAN matching each node) and one client cert were signed by that CA and verified with openssl verify -CAfile against all four leaf certs (all OK).","evidence_ids":["cmd-8bd88600c93b456d","cmd-83102c6aa12246ef","cmd-e6aa249229304572"]},{"id":"req-4","requirement":"Only clients presenting a certificate issued by the cluster CA are accepted for AMQP connections","status":"satisfied","summary":"ssl_options in rabbitmq.config sets verify_peer + fail_if_no_peer_cert=true. Direct tests show a CA-issued client cert completes the TLS handshake successfully, while no client cert and a cert from an unrelated self-signed CA both fail with SSL handshake failure/bad certificate alerts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-dd4fb49746114e6f","cmd-8796808fdac84aa9","cmd-c9a9f6c6c1fb4984","cmd-66a670f377da4beb"]},{"id":"req-5","requirement":"Management interface (15672) reachable only from within the cluster","status":"satisfied","summary":"Management listener is bound to each node's private cluster IP (not 0.0.0.0/loopback), and iptables additionally accepts only the 10.100.106.0/24 cluster subnet on 15672 while dropping everything else. Direct tests confirm node-to-node reachability (200) and a loopback probe on the bound node returning connection failure (000), consistent with the private-IP-only binding.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a13282a7d8a54f79","cmd-3b0f9d26569b4790","cmd-8457164c394e4042","cmd-506db632b0c940b0"]},{"id":"req-6","requirement":"End-to-end proof that the cluster shares real data over the TLS/mTLS configuration","status":"satisfied","summary":"A message published on node1 over mutual-TLS AMQP (using the CA-issued client cert) was consumed on node3, both before and again on node1->node2 after the full restart cycle, confirming genuine cross-node queue state sharing under the configured TLS/auth posture.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b86790fbcd7c4055","cmd-8555eed3f3964b24","cmd-bc9dc5d651d84396","cmd-a8fcf88ae1fe4e88"]},{"id":"req-7","requirement":"Provider-wide restart-evidence protocol executed on every managed node (independent of the public task contract)","status":"satisfied","summary":"Each of node1, node2, node3 has a matching pre-restart baseline (boot_id + healthy cluster/service state), a backgrounded reboot acknowledgement, a directly observed changed boot_id after a connection-refused gap, and post-restart recovery of rabbitmq-server, full 3-node quorum with no partitions, and the management firewall rule (which was lost on reboot and explicitly reapplied/persisted). The fixed after-executor boot-identity snapshot for all three nodes matches the executor-reported post-restart boot_ids exactly, cross-confirming the transition. A post-restart end-to-end TLS publish/consume test also succeeded.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b9493274f9434efe","cmd-507f9fcd0a364657","cmd-f1084004928041e0","cmd-70d1f952bb9148bf","cmd-34a6230666834c09","cmd-c5df6829166942df","cmd-8545b151f5cb49a8","cmd-4611c409795c409e","cmd-5018775d78944f31","cmd-0fc5f17a6e12450a","cmd-30104d4d076949d0","cmd-e888fb1d5cfd46f3","cmd-83a7384516ca4758","cmd-c592ba2b285f41dc","cmd-7ae255cd4e2144ce","cmd-70f4d53b11034c23","cmd-7116cc949a3d471a","cmd-ed20e1c630d845ff","cmd-dfe4182e205a44bd","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node3:boot-identity"]}],"confidence":0.88,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.4,"summary":"Most state-changing commands (package installs, CA/cert generation and distribution, rabbitmq.config, cluster join, iptables rules, cookie distribution) map directly to task requirements or the restart protocol and were not reverted where retention was appropriate. However, several unnecessary/weakening residues remain in the final state: (1) the CA's private key (/tmp/ca/private/ca.key) is left on node1 in /tmp rather than removed after certificate issuance, meaning the master signing key for the whole cluster CA persists outside any protected location (cmd-8bd88600c93b456d, confirmed still present in after_executor node1 temp-files diff); (2) after the post-restart re-test, the executor explicitly widened the client TLS private key's permissions to world-readable ('sudo chmod 644 /tmp/client.key') and left the copy in /tmp on both node1 and node2 (cmd-bc9dc5d651d84396, cmd-180ef3f525af4293), a security-weakening mutation that was not reverted; (3) a 'clustertest' RabbitMQ user with management privileges was created purely for pub/sub verification and left in place rather than removed after testing (cmd-00b2a8b09c5647f7); (4) assorted test scripts and pip/apt logs (tls_test.py, tls_consume.py, pip.log, pika.log, ipt.log) were left under /tmp on multiple nodes, which is low-risk clutter but still unreverted residue from verification activity. None of these were destructive or broad, and the core TLS/cluster/firewall configuration itself is sound, but the leftover CA key and the deliberately loosened permissions on a client private key are meaningful, retained security-relevant residue.","evidence_ids":["cmd-8bd88600c93b456d","cmd-bc9dc5d651d84396","cmd-180ef3f525af4293","cmd-00b2a8b09c5647f7","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"The executor built a 3-node RabbitMQ 3.5.7 cluster with a purpose-built CA, TLS-only AMQP (5671, no plaintext), mutual-TLS client authentication enforced via verify_peer/fail_if_no_peer_cert (verified with positive and negative handshake tests), and a management UI restricted to the cluster's private IPs plus an iptables allow-list for the cluster subnet (verified reachable within the cluster). Cross-node message delivery over mTLS was demonstrated both before and after a full sequential restart of all three nodes, with the restart protocol showing clean baseline/ack/changed-boot-id/recovery transitions for every node, cross-confirmed by the fixed lifecycle boot-identity snapshots. All material task outcomes are satisfied. The main deduction is operational hygiene: the CA's private key and a client private key (the latter deliberately made world-readable) were left behind in /tmp on multiple nodes, along with a leftover test user and scratch test artifacts, none of which were cleaned up.","limitations":["No direct test was captured of an actual off-cluster source IP being blocked from the management port; the restriction is inferred from the private-IP bind plus the iptables DROP rule and a within-cluster positive/negative check, not an external-source negative test.","Sensitive key material (CA private key, and a client private key made world-readable) remains on-disk in /tmp after task completion; this is a hygiene/security concern rather than a functional gap in the stated requirements."]},"ttft_